#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221184 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221401 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48cd517d-be8a-5dc2-91af-fbd19231363c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing GTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/id #uuid "fb9baafb-4eca-593f-ad3e-34744fdfd3f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21684" :question/problem "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $\\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = 4(\\Delta x)^3 + (\\Delta x)^4.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\quad$ With step size $\\Delta x = 0.4,$ the global truncation error is ![](images/d6/d6591c7db007621e1868ad178b09b55025845f041d952272c098635c76d54a83.svg)\n\n$\\quad$ With step size $\\Delta x = 0.2,$ the global truncation error is ![](images/27/2760e0882373e1a374fa21f99b1bb5055e7dd76a64812c2a7a0d340678506433.svg)\n\n$\\quad$ Decreasing from $\\Delta x = 0.4$ to $\\Delta x = 0.2,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of ![](images/2e/2e3028c5348dcee8040ea67df7f90b2a43bbac8c69774981b978ac5dfb2150b0.svg)\n\n$\\quad$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n![](images/f8/f81c63d72e983755776cec5ce37e32be9ba8b9c331bc793434745502596e5886.svg)" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^p\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately $k^p.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.4,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.4) & =4(0.4)^{3}+(0.4)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.064+1\\cdot 0.025\\,6 \\\\  & =0.256+0.025\\,6 \\\\  & =0.281\\,6 \\\\  & ≈0.282.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.2,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.2) & =4(0.2)^{3}+(0.2)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.008+1\\cdot 0.001\\,6 \\\\  & =0.032+0.001\\,6 \\\\  & =0.033\\,6 \\\\  & ≈0.034.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.4}{0.2} = 2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\nk^p = 2^p.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.281\\,6$ to $0.033\\,6.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.281\\,6}{0.033\\,6} = 8.380\\,95\\ldots \\approx  \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{8.381}.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈8.380\\,952… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(8.380\\,95…) \\\\ p & ≈3.067….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{3}.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = 4(\\Delta x)^3 + (\\Delta x)^4\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^3\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221185 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221402 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/id #uuid "c94bf874-2055-5ff3-83e3-b5740a1e14e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-21685" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.24,$ the observed local truncation error is $\\tau(0.24) = 0.8.$\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $\\tau(0.08) = 0.01.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method, rounded to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately $k^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.24$ to $0.08.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.24}{0.08} = 3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\nk^{p+1} = 3^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.8$ to $0.01.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.8}{0.01} = 80.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p+1} & ≈80 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(80) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.987… \\\\ p & ≈2.987….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221186 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/id #uuid "b9b0118d-5238-5644-a3d6-695d8f393efe" :question/math-academy-id "e-21686" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($\\tau$) represent a second-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(\\Delta x) = 3(\\Delta x)^2 + 4(\\Delta x)^5$\n\nII. $\\tau(\\Delta x) = 0.6(\\Delta x)^3 + 2(\\Delta x)^4$\n\nIII. $e(\\Delta x) = 7(\\Delta x)^4 + 5(\\Delta x)^6$" :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O((\\Delta x)^p)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O((\\Delta x)^{p+1})\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let’s check each option:\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(\\Delta x) = 3(\\Delta x)^2 + 4(\\Delta x)^5,$ the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $3(\\Delta x)^2 = 3(\\Delta x)^2.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $\\tau(\\Delta x) = 0.6(\\Delta x)^3 + 2(\\Delta x)^4,$ the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $0.6(\\Delta x)^3 = 0.6(\\Delta x)^{2+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(\\Delta x) = 7(\\Delta x)^4 + 5(\\Delta x)^6,$ the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $7(\\Delta x)^4 = 7(\\Delta x)^4.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221187 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366" :knowledge-point/title "Making Inferences Given the Order of a Numerical Method"}] :question/id #uuid "de3c4be6-9d3d-5dcb-b1cf-567195c4ee63" :question/math-academy-id "e-21687" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $1.$\nSuppose that using step size $\\Delta x = 0.4$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $\\tau(0.4) \\approx 0.24.$\nIf we instead use a step size $\\Delta x = 0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n![](images/5a/5a748823d0f54b92891d55761d2cecdd788bf797dd313173315e7731b6f4d0bc.svg)" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately $k^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\frac{0.4}{0.1} = 4.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $1,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\nk^{1+1} = 4^2 = 16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $\\Delta x = 0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\n\\tau(0.1) \\approx \\frac{0.24}{16} = 0.015.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366" :knowledge-point/title "Making Inferences Given the Order of a Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb49b900-5044-5955-8823-d0d1314a5937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.018" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb49b900-5044-5955-8823-d0d1314a5937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.018" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443175} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c30a36c-6dfd-538d-b000-0d232ead87f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62f01af8-34f7-51b3-af20-ad432a77f51e" :question/math-academy-id "q-333530" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $2.$\nSuppose that using step size $Δx=0.1$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.1)≈0.072.$\nIf we instead use a step size $Δx=0.05,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.05)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.1$ to $0.05.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.1}{0.05}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $2,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}={2}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.05,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.05)≈\\frac{0.072}{4}=0.018.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ecbe65b-19cc-51d7-8182-ccb6871927f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305759} {:answer/id #uuid "6f35542d-7224-54e0-9d5d-8f686b161502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306211} {:answer/id #uuid "c65a1b4b-0a47-5de4-92b7-c6771f8b1af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306996} {:answer/id #uuid "db2110b5-1300-5033-8663-510785a7cb43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307181} {:answer/id #uuid "e1165ddc-eafe-5fed-94c5-61cc04c33c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1165ddc-eafe-5fed-94c5-61cc04c33c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307233} :answer-field/id #uuid "989a0b40-b3ed-53f1-afcd-b799ed9538cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3f43810-818f-5e05-945e-483e5e28efaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-333692" :question/problem "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{2}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=0.3Δx+2(Δx)^{2}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=9(Δx)^{2}+4(Δx)^{5}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.3Δx+2(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3Δx.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=9(Δx)^{2}+4(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is  $9(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221401 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48cd517d-be8a-5dc2-91af-fbd19231363c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing GTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ca02d59-9dac-5356-af9b-e15310a7e10e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443101} {:answer/id #uuid "85b20aa2-f27c-518a-9744-718e4205a4bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443154} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c992ae-d717-5944-94e5-3493912c7021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443166} {:answer/id #uuid "b065544d-d7d1-5aab-89ee-6f5daceb6160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443169} {:answer/id #uuid "de2ff6f1-c803-5c09-964c-a7254d25d91c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b065544d-d7d1-5aab-89ee-6f5daceb6160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443169} :answer-field/id #uuid "e23d9edb-b78e-567f-ad08-a924a47e6c40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba7a8267-d75d-5d4b-bc96-5af23d284353" :question/math-academy-id "q-334541" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.10,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.10)=0.196.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.05,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.05)=0.050.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.10$ to $0.05.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.10}{0.05}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={2}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.196$ to $0.050.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.196}{0.050}=3.92.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈3.92 \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(3.92) \\\\ p & ≈1.9708….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the approximate order of the method is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221401 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48cd517d-be8a-5dc2-91af-fbd19231363c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing GTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29061ed8-03ee-5954-99b1-36ee8a7d846e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443105} {:answer/id #uuid "51897522-282c-5afb-8a2d-f610a69ae795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443125} {:answer/id #uuid "5751dcbe-8a8b-5270-9916-49268913535f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443132} {:answer/id #uuid "b1090be2-8599-5d76-a4a0-6edb0f56303b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443170} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8d57dc-394d-5daa-9001-0b4b29133d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5751dcbe-8a8b-5270-9916-49268913535f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443132} :answer-field/id #uuid "363cd73d-f05d-5d69-8b42-f72895d3771c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34486fa6-cccf-5fd8-a975-d4b80d4ead78" :question/math-academy-id "q-334545" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.12,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.12)=0.410.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.06)=0.026.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.12$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.12}{0.06}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={2}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.410$ to $0.026.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.410}{0.026}=15.769….\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈15.769… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(15.769…) \\\\ p & ≈3.979….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the approximate order of the method is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221401 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48cd517d-be8a-5dc2-91af-fbd19231363c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing GTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2799aa5a-ed9b-5b21-bfd3-2dcb7524fe57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305523} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecc6880-8400-5596-b032-131ea2624d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306369} {:answer/id #uuid "828aa0c9-e28f-5245-9745-37d3d10f96f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306399} {:answer/id #uuid "a81c60b8-ab9f-5ed7-adec-427dc50c769d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306736} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b47cb7-81c5-5c0d-a157-33459420c044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "828aa0c9-e28f-5245-9745-37d3d10f96f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306399} :answer-field/id #uuid "94522b9c-8ce8-51fb-835e-f8705f7e8843" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f94b6c1-fa0e-5c48-970a-a0d605d1856d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334546" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.18,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.18)=0.486.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.06)=0.006.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.18$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.18}{0.06}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={3}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.486$ to $0.006.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.486}{0.006}=81.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈81 \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(81) \\\\ p & ≈4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximate order of the method is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221401 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48cd517d-be8a-5dc2-91af-fbd19231363c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing GTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1629f72c-2b7a-5ad9-9802-4a4c18a43f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443097} {:answer/id #uuid "43cf3c31-8854-53af-adef-a8e2bab7d1e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443119} {:answer/id #uuid "7d685181-3077-58b7-9df9-6de68433f229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443148} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c9f221-8401-5b5d-8ccb-8da7e82c26b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443168} {:answer/id #uuid "f46447fb-7aa1-5e35-a855-5d5b8945d5cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d685181-3077-58b7-9df9-6de68433f229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443148} :answer-field/id #uuid "251effb9-9c02-568e-85ed-f4b7bd54ba26" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443207} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5487e760-2bc5-53ad-bd4e-fd4334efe259" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.008" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443130} {:answer/id #uuid "6604ea4b-bbe7-5e83-a1de-80d7b0224c4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.138" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443140} {:answer/id #uuid "a460527f-d08d-5e8f-9558-fa2fa830502e" :ans