wer.type/math} :answer/value "0.138" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443096} :answer-field/id #uuid "a088a7b0-d0de-5845-8835-029386c41403" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443214} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a508b9f-0d5a-531f-825c-e1abd93fb8bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443093} {:answer/id #uuid "0b656bb6-b726-513c-9a3d-224438a3ecc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443094} {:answer/id #uuid "4443a2a7-05c1-5972-ad6e-f45be9b22e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.812" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443121} {:answer/id #uuid "d149b6a5-96d4-56d2-b34b-fb5c6941b716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.615" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443180} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e36dee-2678-5b5b-9982-81cfba7adeed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24.375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d149b6a5-96d4-56d2-b34b-fb5c6941b716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.615" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443180} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8d5da60-b415-58cd-91b5-f58b2bd119a3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a67eecd4-7182-53b5-9971-e13bae152b30" :question/math-academy-id "q-334561" :question/problem "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.4$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.4) & =5(0.4)^{4}+(0.4)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.0256+1\\cdot 0.010\\,24 \\\\  & =0.128+0.010\\,24 \\\\  & =0.138\\,24 \\\\  & ≈0.138.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.2) & =5(0.2)^{4}+(0.2)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.0016+1\\cdot 0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.008+0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.008\\,32 \\\\  & ≈0.008.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.4}{0.2}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={2}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.138\\,24$ to $0.008\\,32.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.138\\,24}{0.008\\,32}=16.615\\,384…≈16.615.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈16.615\\,384… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(16.615\\,384…) \\\\ p & ≈4.054….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,e(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221401 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48cd517d-be8a-5dc2-91af-fbd19231363c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing GTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2292995f-4795-53f0-bb31-0814de610887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443104} {:answer/id #uuid "665f02dc-14c0-5123-88fb-032a815ccba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.579" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443141} {:answer/id #uuid "8c629b5b-85df-5784-a9c3-23380a9f93bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.667" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443157} {:answer/id #uuid "c73e075e-d5cf-520c-b4a4-968543b00eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.875" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443172} {:answer/id #uuid "ed993786-8a89-51cf-bcfa-dc0e023abeb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.004" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "665f02dc-14c0-5123-88fb-032a815ccba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.579" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443141} :answer-field/id #uuid "22224acc-e8fd-5f6b-987d-4bc060c0fd71" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443206} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47429