nswer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443197} {:answer/id #uuid "f790d854-d14d-5074-bad9-4ec3940b30e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68bf3506-76d5-5c52-9134-2b2746381b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443142} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7969c52-bb2d-55c2-ad55-3063b09bd2c2" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443218} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11681063-6b8a-56d3-bde1-2daa1f9bda0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.166" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443095} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd4afe0-ed39-5fd6-9d3c-ac6d9718d087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.673" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443138} {:answer/id #uuid "80f43a5a-1036-5411-8514-367d88df7b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.924" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443152} {:answer/id #uuid "990ddb7a-0ade-5ec7-bf32-fd750515da52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.154" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443160} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0e1882-c741-559f-8dfb-b2b820290987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.312" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "990ddb7a-0ade-5ec7-bf32-fd750515da52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.154" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443160} :answer-field/id #uuid "db9d26b2-c636-50cd-b971-f41c9e5386dd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec94fc3e-e8fc-535e-a62b-74b6f854910e" :question/math-academy-id "q-334564" :question/problem "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.3,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.6) & =6(0.6)^{3}-(0.6)^{4} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.216-1\\cdot 0.129\\,6 \\\\  & =1.296-0.129\\,6 \\\\  & =1.166\\,4 \\\\  & ≈1.166.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.3) & =6(0.3)^{3}-(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.027-1\\cdot 0.008\\,1 \\\\  & =0.162-0.008\\,1 \\\\  & =0.153\\,9 \\\\  & ≈0.154.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.3.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.3}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={2}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $1.166\\,4$ to $0.153\\,9.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{1.166\\,4}{0.153\\,9}=7.578\\,94…≈7.579.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈7.578\\,94… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(7.578\\,94…) \\\\ p & ≈2.922….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$3.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}\\,⇒\\,e(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{3})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221402 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d85bffd-8755-5e9b-a7b3-89febd886899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443106} {:answer/id #uuid "36fa518b-6582-5224-a8cb-c3138d904c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443108} {:answer/id #uuid "78fccd02-9cc5-55f5-b63f-230bd2ea38ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443145} {:answer/id #uuid "d25d6992-22c7-51c6-8f7a-dcf497256df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443181} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6df07a-d7ae-549b-8810-208c22275136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d25d6992-22c7-51c6-8f7a-dcf497256df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443181} :answer-field/id #uuid "a944373a-a6f7-5cc7-b56a-d45f56447bab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1812d74c-f7ff-5b92-ad0c-d300745fe3f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-334643" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.09,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.09)=0.20.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.06)=0.05.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.09$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.09}{0.06}=1.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(1.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.20$ to $0.05.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.20}{0.05}=4.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.5)^{p+1} & ≈4 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{1.5}⁡(4) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.419… \\\\ p & ≈2.419….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221402 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "516b9685-0ec7-5784-b050-aded8c8a1cc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305943} {:answer/id #uuid "51732cc2-4655-5a0a-a764-680869a446ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305944} {:answer/id #uuid "6801fd43-32da-53c7-958f-9fd853108be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306143} {:answer/id #uuid "818a54c4-98fa-5ddd-8121-3a619a4286a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306389} {:answer/id #uuid "c22ebcd6-6d54-5211-b00c-21fc407fd31d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c22ebcd6-6d54-5211-b00c-21fc407fd31d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306962} :answer-field/id #uuid "82c894fd-10b5-50a4-85f2-09325e510c52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9e60f41-0074-5df6-9afc-69a24a0bbeb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-334644" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.25)=0.50.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.10,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.10)=0.01.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.25$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.25}{0.10}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(2.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.50$ to $0.01.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.50}{0.01}=50.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.5)^{p+1} & ≈50 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(50) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.269… \\\\ p & ≈3.269….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221402 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d2fcee1-789f-5a65-b0c3-dbb43767d387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305422} {:answer/id #uuid "3223be5f-1c23-51da-ac5b-227bc9ab4e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305619} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9568f3-303b-5e68-b773-a6eb6264efec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306339} {:answer/id #uuid "929c322b-6dd4-562d-bdcd-e14b9e67aee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306541} {:answer/id #uuid "b44f66d3-8c24-5e49-a0a1-36983570214c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c9568f3-303b-5e68-b773-a6eb6264efec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306339} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd4d4697-8b4c-5997-bfc4-9a5ec2b0f3a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f92b889e-ac1a-5f7b-b100-034485795a76" :question/math-academy-id "q-334650" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.20,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.20)=0.78.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08)=0.008.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.20$ to $0.08.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.20}{0.08}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(2.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.78$ to $0.008.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.78}{0.008}=97.5.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.5)^{p+1} & ≈97.5 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(97.5) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.99… \\\\ p & ≈3.99….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221402 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a446e26-74cb-5835-b369-58a822cab3a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443099} {:answer/id #uuid "3ceeb595-482c-56a6-b69a-748520742b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443112} {:answer/id #uuid "40e22b30-d2e7-587d-9946-51d20145fa00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443115} {:answer/id #uuid "50918c70-3a77-56fc-afe9-e86bf91b1ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.550" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443124} {:answer/id #uuid "cf28a74b-72cd-5f8f-86bf-d59857f9338a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.048" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf28a74b-72cd-5f8f-86bf-d59857f9338a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.048" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443179} :answer-f