ent :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443144} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb2f318b-0f2a-5b89-a743-9aa784d99c39" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d97bcc9d-8c7b-5d3a-9dd6-550b8b7b1d07" :question/math-academy-id "q-334668" :question/problem "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.3,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.6) & =5(0.6)^{4}+3(0.6)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.129\\,6+3\\cdot 0.077\\,76 \\\\  & =0.648+0.233\\,28 \\\\  & =0.881\\,28 \\\\  & ≈0.881.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.3) & =5(0.3)^{4}+3(0.3)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.008\\,1+3\\cdot 0.002\\,43 \\\\  & =0.0405+0.007\\,29 \\\\  & =0.047\\,79 \\\\  & ≈0.048.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.3.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.3}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.881\\,28$ to $0.047\\,79.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.881\\,28}{0.047\\,79}=18.440\\,67…≈18.441.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p+1} & ≈18.440\\,67… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(18.440\\,67…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.204… \\\\ p & ≈3.204….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$3.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})=O\\,((Δx)^{3+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221402 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "887a3026-9903-56d2-9aa7-26975dc760bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27.294" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443155} {:answer/id #uuid "91659457-b7a8-5a01-a107-ddec07203117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29.571" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443158} {:answer/id #uuid "a62e13f1-93a7-54f9-bfe1-e87ec30413e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19.065" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443164} {:answer/id #uuid "dff182fc-22c0-5b2c-b956-e18ccb5d3fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.231" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443187} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b89532-e6f6-5e27-b7a4-4d11c1214dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32.384" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91659457-b7a8-5a01-a107-ddec07203117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29.571" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443158} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bfc70f9-ba83-5182-b647-bd43911d16c4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443205} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51f35f1c-f301-54e6-9bbc-620544c66c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443127} {:answer/id #uuid "7c212b18-052b-5947-83ed-2750ffa5a330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443146} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf9cf30-7ba4-5601-a3c0-9a8f173d1297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443147} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3222b9-81c4-5af0-93b8-c8fc2af4c060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443178} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0eb53e-c7b8-54bf-ab8f-931682e91192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef0eb53e-c7b8-54bf-ab8f-931682e91192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443192} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bac49a8-a694-5683-a082-922ff0afbdd3" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443209} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16811bb1-a290-5110-9278-74a13897fa65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443098} {:answer/id #uuid "387f547e-35bc-53a4-9409-9ca8ed35c9b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.034" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443109} {:answer/id #uuid "4384b939-3d1e-52fc-b803-94336b68185b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.550" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443118} {:answer/id #uuid "8025e60e-bd13-5838-aff5-73f558fd48aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443150} {:answer/id #uuid "a83e1720-cd01-5745-a4e3-29723928dc38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.994" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a83e1720-cd01-5745-a4e3-29723928dc38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.994" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443167} :answer-field/id #uuid "7912775d-199c-5477-a706-c53543d42878" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443212} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a7dd00c-ee32-5ddb-b80a-7e15b11f8bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.994" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443100} {:answer/id #uuid "4437c035-a236-5585-8ce6-4487ec65e074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443120} {:answer/id #uuid "a66716b2-1c6d-5732-bf74-79e281d42a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.034" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443165} {:answer/id #uuid "e84c2fe4-8b5f-5c9d-bd06-e54ada727e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443189} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8ffd7b-a399-5917-8ac7-1f8bcefc5202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.550" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a66716b2-1c6d-5732-bf74-79e281d42a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.034" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443165} :answer-field/id #uuid "be528cf4-d8cc-5ef0-bc8a-8be45340780c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4879a58-4027-569c-b7a3-4f905f12c0fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-334725" :question/problem "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+(Δx)^{4}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.6) & =4(0.6)^{3}+(0.6)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.216+0.129\\,6 \\\\  & =0.993\\,6 \\\\  & ≈0.994.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.2) & =4(0.2)^{3}+(0.2)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.008+0.001\\,6 \\\\  & =0.033\\,6 \\\\  & ≈0.034.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.2}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={3}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.993\\,6$ to $0.033\\,6.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.993\\,6}{0.033\\,6}=29.571\\,42…≈29.571.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p+1} & ≈29.571\\,42… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(29.571\\,42…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.083… \\\\ p & ≈2.083….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$2.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+(Δx)^{4}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{3})=O\\,((Δx)^{2+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "218a2453-01ac-5477-9e84-010ad313a737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443102} {:answer/id #uuid "5869c9cf-72ad-57b8-9b01-afed018068ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443134} {:answer/id #uuid "6ebae68b-3031-59b4-86e5-efe42825b7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443143} {:answer/id #uuid "f115cc39-13ba-5329-863a-fd3838a1bef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443194} {:answer/id #uuid "f412d79a-33f6-5bcb-b658-0b1fc288cc46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5869c9cf-72ad-57b8-9b01-afed018068ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443134} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8840ee7-0877-5782-9c59-9fed15f7a713" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d0aee14-de07-5ea6-8cb4-30e573b28550" :question/math-academy-id "q-334739" :question/problem "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=0.4Δx+5(Δx)^{2}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=7(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{4}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{2}+0.2(Δx)^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.4Δx+5(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4Δx.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=7(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $7(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{2}+0.2(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $3(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b8cbf73-76e1-54a5-84d4-d80333a4ef75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305721} {:answer/id #uuid "9bdf10d7-6e1c-5467-b614-8a3266c84156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306607} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e8e5bc-4fba-5680-bb8e-d9821151b2d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307083} {:answer/id #uuid "f2940528-333a-58e7-b7e9-e35fa963ea78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307386} {:answer/id #uuid "ff68c2e6-89a9-5261-a203-cad23dd679a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff68c2e6-89a9-5261-a203-cad23dd679a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307507} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7482acd-6813-50d9-954d-0ffe427a7d23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "512c526c-f958-5c94-ad6a-b60bd207799d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334745" :question/problem "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{5}+0.5(Δx)^{6}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{4}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{5}+0.5(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $11(Δx)^{5}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $5$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.2Δx.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "445b4972-2bf0-5719-9a37-bca571bea627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443122} {:answer/id #uuid "5c6e1ed0-06b1-5ca3-ad4a-39554d1e3182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443136} {:answer/id #uuid "60dcb97a-6c3e-59a6-ab57-540ba0016358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443139} {:answer/id #uuid "800f335d-43f8-577e-9911-2a7ac4b4cfc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443149} {:answer/id #uuid "fd06ae41-23c0-58e5-92fd-7205a48af613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd06ae41-23c0-58e5-92fd-7205a48af613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443203} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc230a0b-2414-5af0-ae24-e7a4a94012f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99c7bcc8-5821-518d-9fe0-5a2eb829c883" :question/math-academy-id "q-334746" :question/problem "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a third-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=5(Δx)^{2}+3(Δx)^{3}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=7(Δx)^{3}+(Δx)^{6}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=0.4Δx+9(Δx)^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=5(Δx)^{2}+3(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{2}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=7(Δx)^{3}+(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $7(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.4Δx+9(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4Δx.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "418b4194-ffcb-5bd9-80c5-0907e534d558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443116} {:answer/id #uuid "51d5c0c3-9d4b-5c33-ba7e-37ba2404b976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443126} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c5ba70-5266-5bbc-9058-2e20c6deb40b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443162} {:answer/id #uuid "d35050c1-1a71-58b1-9e7e-7ad043e59fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443182} {:answer/id #uuid "df73a5d1-7721-5994-9aa6-d617ef112c6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51d5c0c3-9d4b-5c33-ba7e-37ba2404b976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443126} :answer-field/id #uuid "e723f15c-67d1-5701-9063-4001a95d8429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd55dc02-570c-5acd-bec7-3aaa43923830" :question/math-academy-id "q-334750" :question/problem "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a first-order numerical method?\n\nI. $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+2(Δx)^{5}$\n\nII. $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3}$\n\nIII. $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{2}+9(Δx)^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+2(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}=6(Δx)^{2+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4(Δx)^{2}=0.4(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{2}+9(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{2}=5(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366" :knowledge-point/title "Making Inferences Given the Order of a Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c564be2-b5d6-576e-b6c5-4b2e22ea2310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.002" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c564be2-b5d6-576e-b6c5-4b2e22ea2310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.002" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305415} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4f564b5-049c-5bd7-a511-bc72af30b334" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e9fb99c-0502-586a-b64c-76d5a0146e7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334781" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$\nSuppose that using step size $Δx=0.1$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.1)≈0.512.$\nIf we instead use a step size $Δx=0.025,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.025)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.1$ to $0.025.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.1}{0.025}=4.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4}={4}^{4}=256.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.025,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.025)≈\\frac{0.512}{256}=0.002.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366" :knowledge-point/title "Making Inferences Given the Order of a Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6479670-90b2-527b-b1f2-f2ac3e1eca3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6479670-90b2-527b-b1f2-f2ac3e1eca3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306854} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab070b56-038b-599b-b680-8ef2d02605e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfc83ddc-db4f-5e46-adbe-84eb415a3d1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334947" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.4$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.4)≈1.024.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.4}{0.1}=4.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4+1}={4}^{5}=1024.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.1)≈\\frac{1.024}{1024}=0.001.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366" :knowledge-point/title "Making Inferences Given the Order of a Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c888821-4abd-556d-946d-6732bb9e8b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.005" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c888821-4abd-556d-946d-6732bb9e8b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.005" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443111} :answer-field/id #uuid "acf8e5b1-b98e-51b0-8a06-878baca2b0a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42270ee1-30c3-599c-b59e-da86dc2e3b89" :question/math-academy-id "q-334952" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.3$ produces local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.3)≈1.215.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.3$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.3}{0.1}=3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4+1}={3}^{5}=243.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.1)≈\\frac{1.215}{243}=0.005.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366" :knowledge-point/title "Making Inferences Given the Order of a Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a8847bb-f2df-592f-a626-453571669151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a8847bb-f2df-592f-a626-453571669151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443135} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8f920a9-df32-5f82-a2de-198e71a776e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf397494-3423-5d84-aaa6-feb204494ce6" :question/math-academy-id "q-334953" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $1.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.3$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.3)≈0.27.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.3$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.3}{0.1}=3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $1,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{1+1}={3}^{2}=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.1)≈\\frac{0.27}{9}=0.03.\n$$"}]}
